Two bugs:
1. `window = min(text.size(), max_len_ + 16)` — only scans the last `max_len_ + 16` bytes, so if the stop string is completed in the beginning of the text (e.g. the answer is 20 chars and stop occurred earlier?), it won't be found. Actually more critically: the stop string can appear anywhere; the window limits search to the tail. For a short answer (< 20 chars) with stops being 10 chars (`</answer>` is 9 chars), max_len_ = 9, window = min(20, 25) = 20, fine. But the 197 cases... Hmm.

2. `at == 0` — only returns match position if the stop string starts at the very beginning of the tail window. If the stop string is found at position > 0 within the tail, it's ignored. That's the main bug: `tail.find(s)` returns the position; the condition `at == 0` only accepts matches at the start of the tail window. So for the stop string to match, it must start exactly at the beginning of the window. That explains "generazione si ferma in 3 casi, tutti con risposte di meno di 20 caratteri" — with short answers (window == text.size() since min(text.size(), 25) — if text.size() < 25, window == text.size(), so tail == text, and `at == 0` requires the stop string to be at position 0... hmm, that's weird too).

Found the bug: in `json_object_end`, when in a string, on encountering `\` it does `continue` — which skips only one char, but the escaped char following the backslash is still processed as a normal char in the loop. Actually wait — the bug: when in_string and c == '\\', it does `continue`, which skips to the next i. So the escaped character (e.g., `\"`) — the `"` after backslash is processed on the next iteration as a normal char: in_string is still true, and c == '"' → in_string = false. That's wrong! For `\"`, the backslash escapes the quote, but the code treats the backslash as "skip" and the quote as ending the string. So the string ends prematurely.

Fix: when in_string and c == '\\', skip the next character (i++ or check c == '\\' and increment). Standard approach:

```cpp
if (in_string) {
    if (c == '\\') { ++i; continue; }
    if (c == '"') in_string = false;
    continue;
}
```

Note: `++i` inside a for loop with `++i` in increment — actually for loop: `for (i...) { ... }` — incrementing i inside, then the loop's `++i` moves past the escaped char. So need `i++` inside and loop does another `i++`, or use while loop. Let me just use `++i` inside and the loop increment will skip the escaped char and land on the one after it. Actually: loop increments i each iteration. If at i we see '\\', we do `++i` → now at escaped char, continue → loop increments → moves to next char after escaped. Correct.

